// unknown_member_returns_null.hv — an unknown member on a module namespace
// resolves to null instead of raising an error.
//
// What this tests: the CURRENT (undesirable) behaviour, pinned so the
// eventual fix has something to change. window.getPid() has never been a
// host function — the documented API is window.pid — so this call must
// resolve to nothing.
//
// Why it is NOT a smoke test: it asserts a defect, not correct
// behaviour. When the language is changed to raise on an unknown module
// member, this script's expectation flips and it should move into the
// smoke suite as a regression test for the NEW behaviour.
//
// Expected result: pid is null, no error is raised, and the downstream
// consumer of that null is what fails:
//
//     process.sendSignal(pid, process.SIGKILL)
//     -> __proc.kill() requires a number PID
//
// That downstream error names the consumer, not the typo, which is the
// whole problem this documents.
//
// Related: docs/known-issues/unknown-member-silently-null.md

pid = window.getPid()
print("pid is: $pid")
if pid == nil {
  print("CONFIRMED: unknown member resolved to null without an error")
} else {
  print("CHANGED: unknown member now raises or returns non-null")
}
